  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Storytelling around the fire, **okugera enfumo**, has carried Ganda wisdom for generations. Stories have their own opening, linking and closing words.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Olwatuuka olwali olumu…", "base": "Once upon a time, one day…" },
      { "target": "Waaliwo omusajja omu.", "base": "There was a man." },
      { "target": "Awo n’agenda mu kibira.", "base": "Then he went into the forest." },
      { "target": "N’alaba empologoma!", "base": "And he saw a lion!" },
      { "target": "Ekyavaamu, n’addayo awaka.", "base": "In the end, he went back home." },
      { "target": "Enfumo yange ekomye awo.", "base": "My story ends there." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "story-openings",
        "title": "Opening a story",
        "body": [
          "Start with **Olwatuuka** (it happened) or **Olwatuuka olwali olumu** (it happened one day), then set the scene with **Waaliwo** (there was): **Waaliwo omusajja omu** (there was a man)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "narrative-ne",
        "title": "And then: ne-",
        "body": [
          "In a story, each new event is a verb with **ne-** in front: **n’agenda** (and he went), **n’alaba** (and he saw), **n’agamba** (and he said). It works like the far past, chaining events one after another.",
          "Use **Awo** (then) to move the story along, and **Ekyavaamu** (in the end) to finish it."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Stories at night",
        "body": [
          "Folktales were told in the evening after supper, often by grandparents. Many feature **Wakayima** the hare, a clever trickster. You will meet him in Unit 40."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "stories": [
    {
      "id": "amaras-story",
      "title": "Amara tells a story",
      "unlockAfter": "u35s3l3",
      "cast": ["amara", "emma"],
      "summary": "Amara tells Emma a folktale about a boy and a lion.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "line": { "speaker": "emma", "target": "Amara, ngerera enfumo!", "base": "Amara, tell me a story!" },
          "gloss": { "ngerera": "tell for me" }
        },
        {
          "line": {
            "speaker": "amara",
            "target": "Kale nno. Olwatuuka olwali olumu, waaliwo omulenzi omu.",
            "base": "Well then. Once upon a time, there was a boy."
          },
          "gloss": { "omulenzi": "boy" }
        },
        {
          "line": {
            "speaker": "amara",
            "target": "Awo n’agenda mu kibira okunoonya enku.",
            "base": "Then he went into the forest to look for firewood."
          },
          "gloss": { "okunoonya": "to look for", "enku": "firewood" }
        },
        { "question": "Why did the boy go into the forest?", "options": ["To find firewood", "To hunt", "To play football"], "answer": 0 },
        { "line": { "speaker": "emma", "target": "N’alaba ki?", "base": "And what did he see?" } },
        { "line": { "speaker": "amara", "target": "N’alaba empologoma nnene nnyo!", "base": "He saw a very big lion!" } },
        { "line": { "speaker": "emma", "target": "Ayi! N’adduka?", "base": "Oh! Did he run?" } },
        {
          "line": { "speaker": "amara", "target": "Nedda. N’atuula wansi, n’ayimba oluyimba.", "base": "No. He sat down and sang a song." }
        },
        {
          "question": "What did the boy do when he saw the lion?",
          "options": ["He ran away", "He sat and sang a song", "He climbed a tree"],
          "answer": 1
        },
        {
          "line": {
            "speaker": "amara",
            "target": "Empologoma n’ewuliriza, n’ewummula, n’egenda.",
            "base": "The lion listened, rested and went away."
          }
        },
        {
          "line": {
            "speaker": "amara",
            "target": "Ekyavaamu, omulenzi n’addayo awaka n’enku. Enfumo yange ekomye awo.",
            "base": "In the end, the boy went home with the firewood. My story ends there."
          }
        },
        {
          "question": "How does Amara end the story?",
          "options": ["Enfumo yange ekomye awo.", "Olwatuuka olwali olumu.", "Waaliwo omusajja."],
          "answer": 0,
          "optionsLang": "target"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
